From: Mahdi Taziki <61482609+Mhd-tz@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] msvcrt, ntdll: Stop at the target handler's scope during a target unwind.

During a target unwind (EXCEPTION_TARGET_UNWIND), x64 __C_specific_handler walks the function's scope table and calls
the termination handlers of the scopes it leaves. With Clang's nested SEH layout, the continuation of the handled
scope can lie outside the PC range of an enclosing __finally, so the walk skipped the handled scope and ran the
enclosing finally too early; normal continuation then ran it a second time. In C++ code that unregisters the same
catch frame twice: OGRE's Sample Browser crashed in ucrtbase (_FindAndUnlinkFrame, NULL+8) while catching an ordinary
exception. Stop the walk at the scope whose JumpTarget is the unwind target, as Windows does, in both copies of the
scope walker (msvcrt/ucrtbase and ntdll).

Provenance (recorded by AnvilMac, 2026-10-16):
  Written for AnvilMac (investigation by OpenAI Codex, reviewed by Claude Code); not submitted upstream yet (draft 5 in
  AnvilMac's notes). Base: wine-11.19, commit 455e3509b98a.
  Files: dlls/msvcrt/except.c, dlls/ntdll/unwind.c (Wine, LGPL-2.1-or-later).
  License of this change: LGPL-2.1-or-later. Copyright 2026 Mahdi Taziki.
  Checked: five native nested-SEH scenarios pass through both handlers (two fail per handler without it); Wine's UCRT
  C++ tests pass (41 x64, 36 i386); OGRE's original catch succeeds; hands-off input probes unchanged (54/54).

--- a/dlls/msvcrt/except.c
+++ b/dlls/msvcrt/except.c
@@ -1282,6 +1282,9 @@
         {
             if (pc < base + table->ScopeRecord[i].BeginAddress) continue;
             if (pc >= base + table->ScopeRecord[i].EndAddress) continue;
+            if ((rec->ExceptionFlags & EXCEPTION_TARGET_UNWIND) &&
+                dispatch->TargetIp == base + table->ScopeRecord[i].JumpTarget)
+                break;
             if (table->ScopeRecord[i].JumpTarget) continue;
 
             if (rec->ExceptionFlags & EXCEPTION_TARGET_UNWIND &&
